# What must never enter the build context.
#
# Without this file, `docker build` uploads everything in the directory to the
# daemon — including a local .env and a 200 MB node_modules built for the wrong
# platform. The .env line is the one that matters: a secret that reaches the
# build context can end up in an image layer, and an image layer is forever.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

node_modules/
npm-debug.log*

.git/
.github/
.gitignore

# The tests are not part of the running service.
test/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT DIRECTORY, FILE BY FILE.
#
# This said `contract/`, and the comment above it said the contract fixtures
# "are not part of the running service". That stopped being true when
# src/domain/market.js began reading contract/market-config.js with readFileSync
# AT IMPORT TIME, and the result was a build that failed on the line that copies
# it:
#
#     COPY contract/market-config.js: "/contract/market-config.js": not found
#
# The file was in the repository and named in the Dockerfile. It was simply never
# in the build context.
#
# NAMED INDIVIDUALLY RATHER THAN `contract/*` PLUS AN EXCEPTION.
#
# A `!` exception inside an excluded directory is the kind of thing that works
# in one builder and not the next, and I could not run a real docker build here
# to find out which this is. A failed deploy has already cost time once; listing
# the five files that must stay out needs no negation and cannot be read two
# ways. test/deploy.test.js enumerates this directory and fails if a NEW file
# appears that is neither excluded here nor the one file the service reads — so
# the cost of being explicit is paid by a test, not by a surprise.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
contract/backend.md
contract/homepage-feed.html
contract/homepage-intake.js
contract/js43.js
contract/netlify.toml

*.md
!README.md

.DS_Store
coverage/

# COMPILED OUTPUT, WHICH IS BUILT INSIDE THE IMAGE AND NEVER SENT TO IT.
#
# The builder stage compiles src/ into /app/dist and the runtime stage copies
# it across with COPY --from. A dist/ left over from a local build has no
# business in the build context: at best it is dead weight, at worst it is
# stale output that looks like the current one.
dist/
